  "content": {
    "id": "p124-practice-02-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p124-practice-02-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "True or False?",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-02-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\lvert x-3\\rvert=\\lvert 3-x\\rvert$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. By symmetry, reversing the order of a difference does not change its absolute value. The expressions $x-3$ and $3-x$ are negatives of each other.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-02-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\lvert x-1\\rvert=1-x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. For $x=2$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lvert 2-1\\rvert&=1\\\\1-2&=-1\\end{align*}$$\nThe two sides are different.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-02-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\lvert 5x\\rvert=5\\lvert x\\rvert$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. By the multiplication property,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lvert 5x\\rvert&=\\lvert 5\\rvert\\lvert x\\rvert\\\\\\lvert 5x\\rvert&=5\\lvert x\\rvert\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-02-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\lvert x\\rvert^{2}=x^{2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. If $x\\geq 0$, then $\\lvert x\\rvert=x$, so $\\lvert x\\rvert^2=x^2$. If $x<0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lvert x\\rvert^2&=(-x)^2\\\\\\lvert x\\rvert^2&=x^2\\end{align*}$$\nThe equality holds in both cases.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-02-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\lvert -3x\\rvert=3x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. For $x=-1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lvert -3(-1)\\rvert&=3\\\\3(-1)&=-3\\end{align*}$$\nThe two sides are different. The multiplication property gives $\\lvert -3x\\rvert=3\\lvert x\\rvert$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-02-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$\\sqrt{x^{2}}=x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. For $x=-1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{(-1)^2}&=\\sqrt{1}\\\\\\sqrt{(-1)^2}&=1\\end{align*}$$\nThis differs from $x=-1$. In general, $\\sqrt{x^2}=\\lvert x\\rvert$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-02-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$\\lvert ab\\rvert=\\lvert a\\rvert\\lvert b\\rvert$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. This is the multiplication property of absolute value, which holds for all real $a$ and $b$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p124-practice-02-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$\\lvert a+b\\rvert=\\lvert a\\rvert+\\lvert b\\rvert$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. For $a=1$ and $b=-1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lvert 1+(-1)\\rvert&=0\\\\\\lvert 1\\rvert+\\lvert -1\\rvert&=2\\end{align*}$$\nThe two sides are different.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
